# Group Chat

How to write in a group chat (several agents + {{ operator_name }} in one room). You get
here in two ways: a **round brief** from the room engine (system message, thread
`kind=group`), or {{ operator_name }} @-mentions you directly. Same rules either way.

## The one rule

**Short and friendly in the room, the details in a document.** {{ operator_name }} reads
the room like a chat with colleagues — he is an IT person, not a developer. He
must understand every line without opening anything. Everything that needs
scrolling belongs in an attached document, not in the room.

## Writing a contribution

- **Line 1 = your key sentence in bold.** It is also the room's preview line
  (sidebar, notifications). One sentence, no greeting, no "I looked into…".
- Then **at most ~10 short lines**: bullets with a bold label
  (`- **Why:** …`, `- **Risk:** …`, `- **Source:** https://…`).
  No paragraph longer than two sentences.
- **Numbers and comparisons as a table** (max ~6 rows) — the room renders
  GFM markdown: bold, lists, tables, code.
- **Simple language.** Technical terms only when needed, then explained in a
  few words in brackets. Direct and warm, like a good colleague.
- **One claim = one source URL.** The bare link is enough, no quote block.
- Don't @-mention other members in a round — the engine collects every
  contribution and hands it on. Answer {{ operator_name }} directly if he asked you.
- In round 2+ with nothing new to add: reply exactly `PASS`. Passing is a
  full answer; mere agreement is not a contribution.

Send multi-line text via heredoc on stdin — a `"…"` argument would break on
pipes and newlines:

```bash
mc msg --thread <thread-id> - <<'EOF'
**DFlash2 is the faster engine — 424 tok/s at 16 streams.**
- **Why:** measured with the standard bench, same prompt, same context.
- **Risk:** needs the `--max-mamba-cache-size 80` flag or it crashes at start.
- **Source:** https://…

| Engine | tok/s @16 | context |
|---|---|---|
| DFlash2 | 424 | 262k |
| vLLM | 198 | 1.0M |
EOF
```

The thread id is in the brief (after `--thread`) or in the footer of the
message you received (`[thread <id> · seq …]`).

## Uploading a document (details, evidence, full results)

Write the long version to a file and attach it to your contribution. The room
shows it as a tile; `.md` and `.txt` open right inside the chat, rendered.

```bash
cat > /tmp/bench-report.md <<'EOF'
# Bench report — DFlash2 vs vLLM
… full method, all numbers, raw logs, open questions …
EOF
mc msg --thread <thread-id> --attach /tmp/bench-report.md - <<'EOF'
**DFlash2 wins — full numbers in the attached report.**
- **Why:** 2.1× faster at 16 streams, same quality on HumanEval.
- **Risk:** one flag is mandatory, see report §3.
EOF
```

- Attach whenever your evidence is longer than the ~10-line budget: measurements,
  logs, code diffs, screenshots (`--attach shot.png`), comparisons.
- Name the file by its content (`bench-report.md`, not `out.md`). Any file
  type works; images show as previews.
- One document per contribution is plenty — put several results in one file
  with headings rather than attaching five files.

## If you are the lead

After each round you write the verdict — same short format, starting with
exactly one marker: `ZIEL ERREICHT: …`, `WEITER: …` or `FRAGE AN OPERATOR: …`.
The full synthesis (sources, dissent, open points) goes into the group's
**result document**, not into the room:

```bash
cat > /tmp/result.md <<'EOF'
# … living result document …
EOF
mc group-doc <group-id> --file /tmp/result.md
```

Keep sources AND dissent in it; don't smooth anything over. Members' attached
reports are your raw material — link or summarise them there.
